{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787157468,"nonce":"DEzkkBZRKlkkzK6HaEvOvE1H/80=","message":"**Corrections to comment #2, before anyone builds from it. Two of its claims are wrong and one open question in it turns out to be a decision, not a detail.**\n\n⛔ **\"The digest row gains a coverage fact it can render\" is FALSE, and it was my sentence.** Bounded lookback adds no column — it changes the WHERE clause and adds dedupe state scoped to `L`. Nothing in it records the span a given digest covered. So the claim at the end of comment #2 that this unblocks `78388fb` by giving the row something to render does not follow: if a digest is to render its own span, that is a **separate column somebody has to name**, and it should be decided as part of this work rather than assumed to fall out of it.\n\n⛔ **The reconciliation detector cannot be the plain SQL anti-join I described.** I wrote \"a periodic anti-join for Cases whose event time is older than `now − L` and which appear in no digest\". That query cannot be written: a Case appears in no digest either because it was missed OR because **no policy matches it at all**, and matching is decided in Go — `Policy.Matches` is fed a label map at four call sites (`notify.go:291`, `digest.go:255`, `view.go:593`, and the digest path), not in SQL. So the detector has to be a per-(policy) fold over the candidate window reusing the same matching code the tick uses, and its cost is therefore closer to a tick than to a cheap query. Still worth building — the point stands that a visible number beats an invisible hole — but it is not free and it must not diverge from the tick's own matcher, or it will report phantom gaps.\n\n⚠️ **AND THE ONE THAT NEEDS A DECISION: the lookback clamp collides with outage catch-up.** `MaxDigestBackfill` exists so that a missed tick still emits the windows it owed, and `TestAMissedTickWritesAtMostSixDigestsAndSaysWhatItDropped` asserts exactly that — six digests for a 12:40–13:30 gap, plus a log saying what was dropped. A single `L` used as both \"how late a Case may arrive\" and \"how far back we look\" would silently discard the gap after an outage longer than `L`: oto would send nothing for it, and `MaxDigestBackfill`'s stated purpose would be discarded without being argued against.\n\n**These are two different numbers doing two different jobs:**\n\n```\n  L                    how late a CASE may commit and still be counted\n                       — sized against plan-to-commit latency (seconds)\n  MaxDigestBackfill    how many owed WINDOWS a recovered tick still emits\n                       — sized against outage length (many windows)\n```\n\nConflating them is the mistake; `L` is small and about write visibility, backfill is large and about oto being down. The bounded-lookback ruling stands with `L` as the lateness term ONLY, and `MaxDigestBackfill` keeps its own job untouched — but that needs saying out loud, because comment #2 did not distinguish them and an implementer reading it would reasonably use one constant for both.\n\n⚠️ **One more clause the ruling cannot satisfy as written.** `a8a4010`'s Done-when requires that if the answer is a lag, \"the lag is a named constant **with the gap distribution it was sized against**\". No column in the tree can measure commit lateness: `alert_cases.started_at` is stamped app-side before the transaction, and the only DB-stamped column is `created_at DEFAULT now()`, which is transaction-START time and so moves the WRONG WAY — the slower the commit, the earlier the stamp. So the distribution is not measurable today, and `L` is chosen rather than derived. Either that clause relaxes to \"chosen, with the reasoning recorded\", or a `clock_timestamp()`-stamped column is added first — which is close to the visibility mechanism the ruling declined, so relaxing the clause is the cheaper answer.\n\n*Provenance: the two corrections above are mine, reasoned from `Policy.Matches` being Go-side and from bounded lookback adding no column. `MaxDigestBackfill`, the backfill test's name and line range, and the `started_at`/`created_at` clock facts come from a mapping pass over the digest code that I have NOT read myself — treat them as leads to confirm at the first edit, not as verified.*\n\n**Nothing built.** This work is behind `7570090`, which the owner has just prioritised.","files":null}]}